{"created": "2026-02-21T03:02:25.141136+00:00", "modified": "2026-03-08T11:59:53.372956+00:00", "id": 18719033, "conceptrecid": "18719032", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.18719033", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.18719032", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18719033", "metadata": {"title": "Operational Alignment of Aurora-Lens with OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible AI (2026)", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.18719033", "publication_date": "2026-02-21", "description": "<p>This report maps Aurora-Lens, a deterministic transport-layer AI governance proxy, to the OECD (2026) Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible AI. The OECD framework establishes six procedural Responsible Business Conduct (RBC) steps but does not prescribe runtime enforcement mechanisms. This document demonstrates how Aurora-Lens operationalises those expectations through admissibility gating, deterministic intervention actions (PASS, FORCE_REVISE, HARD_STOP), and a tamper-evident audit ledger.</p>\n<p>The architecture is grounded in a Persistent Existence Frame (PEF), a non-temporal state substrate that ensures invariant governance state across interactions. This property enables replayable decision states and mechanically verifiable audit reconstruction, satisfying OECD requirements for traceability, documentation, and reproducible remediation.</p>\n<p>Aurora-Lens operates between applications and large language model (LLM) providers without modifying underlying models. The report positions deterministic transport-layer governance as an enforceable implementation pathway for AI due diligence obligations in regulated environments.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Stokes, Margaret", "affiliation": null, "orcid": "0009-0004-6422-4174"}], "keywords": ["AI Governance", "responsible AI", "AI governance layer", "LLM hallucination detection", "EU AI Act compliance"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.18653120", "relation": "isSupplementTo", "resource_type": "publication", "scheme": "doi"}], "dates": [{"type": "issued"}], "version": "1.0", "references": ["Revised: AI governance layer, LLM hallucination detection, EU AI Act compliance, OECD AI due diligence, healthcare AI audit, financial services AI compliance, AI audit trail, high-stakes AI deployment, AI accountability, regulated industries AI safety, AI governance proxy, hallucination prevention, AI compliance framework, tamper-evident audit log, AI risk management"], "language": "eng", "custom": {"code:codeRepository": "https://pypi.org/project/aurora-lens/", "code:developmentStatus": {"id": "active", "title": {"en": "Active"}}}, "resource_type": {"title": "Report", "type": "publication", "subtype": "report"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "18719032"}}]}}, "title": "Operational Alignment of Aurora-Lens with OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible AI (2026)", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18719033", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/18719033", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/18719033?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18719033", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18719033", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.18719033", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18719033/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18719032", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/18719032", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18719032", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.18719032", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:18719033/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:18719033/sequence/default", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18719033/files", "media_files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18719033/media-files", "thumbnails": {"10": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:18719033:aurora_lens_oecd